  "query_preview": "..\nBroad upper level ridging will persist across the inland\nNorthwest through the remainder of this week. The ridge axis \njust offshore tonight will move to the Washington coast Thursday\nand over Eastern Washington Friday resulting in a warming air \nmass. Highs will be in the upper 80s to mid 90s Thursday and\nFriday. Some valleys near Lewiston could have triple digit \nhighs Thursday or Friday. The temperature forecast is tricky \nthough since the smoke will be thick enough in some areas to \nknock off a few/several degrees from the potential/expected \nhighs. This is most likely Thursday in the central Washington \nvalleys. Low temperatures will be a little warmer due to the \nthick smoke, in the mid 50s to mid 60s. HeatRisk increases to \nmoderate across most of the area with central valley locations \nnearing major HeatRisk if the temperature forecasts pan out. We \nthink the smoke will keep this from being excessive, so we're \nnot planning any heat advisories at this time. \n\nThe upper ridge will move eastward on Friday. Flow aloft then\nbecomes zonal this weekend into early next week. The biggest \nimpact from this pattern change will be an increase in winds as\nshort wave disturbances moving through increase the pressure \ngradient. Winds do remain mostly light before Friday with the \nexception of increasing westerlies along the east slopes of the \nCascades Thursday night. West to southwest winds begin to pick \nup during the day Friday, ease briefly Friday night before \npicking back up again Saturday. With continued warm and very dry\nconditions, this will renew the concern for elevated to \ncritical fire weather conditions. Fire Weather Watches have\nbeen issued for breezy westerly winds and low relative humidity \nboth Friday and Saturday. The strongest winds are most likely \nto remain confined to portions of central Washington including \nthe east Cascade valleys and the Waterville Plateau, but also\nLower Palouse and the Snake River Valley. There is a 50-80% \nchance of wind gusts up to 30-35 mph in those locations in \ncentral Washington Friday and Saturday. Elsewhere in the Basin \nand into Eastern Washington and portions of the northern \npanhandle, gusts in the 15-25 mph are the most likely outcome.\nRH values will likely drop to 10-20% with some single digit\nreadings possible for a few hours during the hottest part of the\ndays. These winds will very likely not be as strong as what was\nobserved on Aug 1, especially east of Grant and Douglas \ncounties. High temperatures remain in the low to mid 90s \nSaturday with lows in the upper 50s to mid 60s. Limited \nRH recovery is expected during the overnights.\n\nConditions Sunday into early next week look to remain under the\ninfluence of zonal flow with weak shortwaves moving through at\ntimes. This will allow for temperatures to cool down closer to \nclimatological normals. No precipitation is currently in the\nforecast with all of it staying north of the US/Canada border.  \nWinds will remain diurnally driven with the potential for some \nlocally breezy winds through the Cascade gaps. -Spilde",
